<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Pereyaslavl</h1><p class="st-meta">1054 CE – 1239 CE · capital Pereyaslavl</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The frontier principality of Kievan Rus' facing the steppe, home base of Vladimir Monomakh in his wars against the Cumans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslavl</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trubizh">Trubizh</a> river, was one of the oldest towns of Rus'. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> died in 1054 he left it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-i-of-kiev">Vsevolod</a>, and it became a principality of its own, third in rank after Kiev and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lying on the edge of the steppe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslavl</a> was a military land of fortresses and earthen walls defending Rus' against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> ruled here from 1094 to 1113 and led campaigns deep into the steppe. The town was briefly the seat of a metropolitan in the 11th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Usually held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Monomakh</a>'s descendants as a stepping stone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, the principality declined in the 12th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslavl</a> in 1239, and the land later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. The town, now Pereiaslav, is where the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1654.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-pereyaslavl.jpg" alt="Principality of Pereyaslavl" width="320" height="235"><figcaption>Image: Das steinerne Herz, Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Triangle_of_Kyiv_Chernihiv_Pereyaslavl_1132.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1054 CE – 1239 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pereyaslavl</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vsevolod I, Vladimir Monomakh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pereiaslav Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=principality-of-pereyaslavl" aria-label="Principality of Pereyaslavl on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5400 436 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Pereyaslavl lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5720 692L5697 681M5720 692L7559 780"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5720 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5720 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=principality-of-pereyaslavl">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

